 ## Overview
-Spherical Harmonics (SH) is a family of techniques that represent directional functions on the unit sphere using a fixed, non-learnable basis of orthogonal functions [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently storing and computing spherical integrals (such as lighting or visibility) by converting them into algebraic operations on coefficient vectors [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by using a global, analytic basis rather than localized kernels (like Spherical Gaussians) or differentiable neural backpropagation (like Ray Tracing Backward Pass), trading high-frequency accuracy for computational speed and gradient compatibility suitable for optimization tasks [S1, unofficial].
+Spherical Harmonics (SH) is a family of methods that represent directional functions, such as radiance or lighting, on the unit sphere using a linear combination of orthogonal basis functions [S1, S3, unofficial]. This representation enables efficient rotation, integration, and compression of high-dimensional directional data, solving the problem of expensive Monte Carlo integration in volume rendering by replacing it with algebraic operations on coefficient vectors [S1, unofficial]. Unlike general spherical basis methods, SH uses a fixed, non-learnable basis, distinguishing it from alternatives like Spherical Gaussians (SG) which use learnable kernels [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Shared principles
-The family relies on the mathematical properties of Spherical Harmonics, which form a complete basis of functions mapping the unit sphere $S^2$ to the complex numbers $\mathbb{C}$ [S1, unofficial]. In graphics, real-valued bases are used to represent directional functions like radiance, visibility, or BRDFs [S2, unofficial].
